From 1e25e167ae742a2b0b4d2ca82e23800bab1f2cad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 13:32:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ci: build scala and kotlin under their newer compilers too

Both ports were made to compile under two major versions of their language,
because a machine that also builds voxgig/struct carries the newer ones:

  #29  kotlin  four call sites spelled so 1.3.31 and 2.x both accept them
  #30  scala   `export` backticked, companion applies made explicit, and a
               Makefile that probes for the flags and the run command

Nothing on GitHub built the newer half of either. `port` installs what
ubuntu's archive carries -- scala 2.11.12 and kotlin 1.3.31 -- so a later
change could break scala 3 or kotlin 2 compatibility and every check here
would stay green. That is the same shape of gap as a port missing from
`port`'s matrix: THE GATE STAYS GREEN BECAUSE THE CASE IS ABSENT FROM IT.

`newer-toolchain` adds the missing half, two lanes, pinned release archives
in the style dart, swift, zig and lean already use here:

  scala   scala3-3.9.0.tar.gz              from github.com/scala/scala3
  kotlin  kotlin-compiler-2.2.0.zip        from github.com/JetBrains/kotlin

kotlin 2.2.0 is what voxgig/struct's kotlin port builds with, so the two
repositories now agree on the newer end as well as differing at the older one.

THE PORTS NEED NOTHING ADDED. Both Makefiles already take the compiler and
the runner as overridable variables and probe for what they got, so each lane
is the ordinary `make build/test/inspect` with PATH pointing at the newer
toolchain. That was the point of the probes, and this is what collects on it.

Both lanes were run before this was written, with the same archives the job
fetches:

  scala   19 files compile; 572 corpus entries across 19 sections, all pass
          inspect: Scala compiler version 3.9.0
  kotlin  19 files compile; 572 corpus entries across 19 sections, all pass
          inspect: kotlinc-jvm 2.2.0

The scala lane is also the first execution of the Scala CLI arm of #30's run
probe. The real 3.9.0 launcher answers `scala --version` with

    Scala code runner version: 1.16.0
    Scala version (default): 3.9.0

-- no mention of "Scala CLI" -- which is why that probe asks whether `run` is
a subcommand instead of reading the banner.

The two apt comments above now say that 2.11 and 1.3.31 are the OLDEST
compilers these ports must satisfy rather than the only ones, and name the job
that checks the other end.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01S5zKiMP1i1FRDZ6pzgzrUp
---
 .github/workflows/ci.yml | 72 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 68 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/.github/workflows/ci.yml b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
index 7bfaf66..9994820 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/ci.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
@@ -160,12 +160,17 @@ jobs:
           unzip -q /tmp/dartsdk.zip -d /opt
           echo /opt/dart-sdk/bin >> "$GITHUB_PATH"
       # kotlin is in ubuntu's archive; the package is the compiler and the
-      # stdlib jar beside it, which is all the port's Makefile looks for.
+      # stdlib jar beside it, which is all the port's Makefile looks for. As
+      # with scala, the archive's 1.3.31 is the oldest compiler this port must
+      # satisfy rather than the only one - `newer-toolchain` below has the
+      # other half.
       - if: matrix.port == 'kotlin'
         run: sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y kotlin
-      # scala likewise. The archive carries 2.11, which is what this port is
-      # written to - `make inspect-scala` prints the version it got, so a newer
-      # one arriving is visible rather than silent.
+      # scala likewise. The archive carries 2.11, which is the OLDEST compiler
+      # this port must satisfy - not the only one. It also builds under scala
+      # 3, and `newer-toolchain` below is what checks that half.
+      # `make inspect-scala` prints the version it got, so a newer one
+      # arriving is visible rather than silent.
       - if: matrix.port == 'scala'
         run: sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y scala
       # swift is not, so this is the release tarball from swift.org at a PINNED
@@ -238,3 +243,62 @@ jobs:
       - run: make build-${{ matrix.port }}
       - run: make test-${{ matrix.port }}
       - run: make inspect-${{ matrix.port }}
+
+  # THE SECOND TOOLCHAIN, for the two ports that must compile under more than
+  # one major version of their language.
+  #
+  # `port` above installs what ubuntu's archive carries - scala 2.11.12 and
+  # kotlin 1.3.31 - and that is deliberate: they are the OLDEST compilers
+  # these ports must still satisfy. But an old compiler is only half of a
+  # compatibility claim, and until this job existed the other half was checked
+  # by nobody. Both ports were made to build under two compilers (#29 kotlin,
+  # #30 scala) because a machine that also builds voxgig/struct carries the
+  # newer ones; with the newer half unbuilt on GitHub, a later change could
+  # break it while `make check` stayed green. That is the same shape of gap as
+  # a port missing from `port`'s matrix: THE GATE STAYS GREEN BECAUSE THE CASE
+  # IS ABSENT FROM IT.
+  #
+  # NEITHER TOOLCHAIN IS IN THE ARCHIVE, so both are PINNED release archives -
+  # the same trade dart, swift, zig and lean already take in this file, and for
+  # the same reason every action here is pinned to a SHA. An unpinned installer
+  # would put this job's meaning outside the repository.
+  #
+  # THE PORTS NEED NOTHING ADDED FOR THIS. Both Makefiles already take the
+  # compiler and the runner as overridable variables and probe for what they
+  # got, so a lane is the ordinary `make build/test/inspect` with PATH pointing
+  # at the newer toolchain. If that ever stops being true, fix the Makefile
+  # rather than special-casing it here.
+  newer-toolchain:
+    name: ${{ matrix.port }} (newer toolchain)
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    strategy:
+      fail-fast: false
+      matrix:
+        port: [scala, kotlin]
+    steps:
+      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7
+      # scala 3, from the scala3 distribution tarball. `bin/scala` in this
+      # archive is the Scala CLI runner that scala 3.5 replaced the old one
+      # with, which is exactly why the port probes for a `run` subcommand
+      # instead of matching a version banner: this launcher reports itself as
+      # `Scala code runner version: 1.16.0` and never says "Scala CLI" at all.
+      - if: matrix.port == 'scala'
+        run: |
+          curl -fsSL -o /tmp/scala3.tar.gz \
+            https://github.com/scala/scala3/releases/download/3.9.0/scala3-3.9.0.tar.gz
+          sudo mkdir -p /opt/scala3
+          sudo tar xzf /tmp/scala3.tar.gz -C /opt/scala3 --strip-components=1
+          echo /opt/scala3/bin >> "$GITHUB_PATH"
+      # kotlin 2.x, from the compiler zip. The port's Makefile locates
+      # kotlin-stdlib.jar beside the compiler rather than fetching it, and this
+      # archive has the same bin/../lib layout ubuntu's package does, so the
+      # lookup needs no help.
+      - if: matrix.port == 'kotlin'
+        run: |
+          curl -fsSL -o /tmp/kotlin.zip \
+            https://github.com/JetBrains/kotlin/releases/download/v2.2.0/kotlin-compiler-2.2.0.zip
+          sudo unzip -q /tmp/kotlin.zip -d /opt
+          echo /opt/kotlinc/bin >> "$GITHUB_PATH"
+      - run: make build-${{ matrix.port }}
+      - run: make test-${{ matrix.port }}
+      - run: make inspect-${{ matrix.port }}
-- 
